ecm_interface: treat tag_8021q DSA user ports as VLAN uppers of the conduit

A DSA switch driven by a tag_8021q tagger talks to its CPU port in plain
802.1Q: a standalone user port carries its own VID, the ports of a
VLAN-unaware bridge share the bridge's VID. On the wire a user port is
therefore an 802.1Q sub-interface of the conduit, and this is how the
accelerated path has to see it.

ECM did not: with ECM_INTERFACE_DSA_ENABLE off (the code under it is a
stub - the VID it writes is always 0 - and it uses pre-6.7 DSA names), a
DSA user port was a plain ethernet leaf. When the port has an NSS
interface number of its own (a firmware VLAN interface bound to the
port, which is what the qca-dsa-nss glue creates) the rule names that
number as its endpoint, the firmware accepts the rule, counts the
packets as transmitted, and none of them reach the GMAC. Measured on
IPQ5018 + QCA8337 with the qca-8021q tagger: every TCP flow through the
router completed its handshake on the host and stalled the moment ECM
pushed it to the firmware; 3/3 downloads dead, GMAC TX counter moving
only by the host's own frames.

Now a user port whose switch has a tag_8021q context is a VLAN iface:
the tag is the tagger's VID for the port (or its bridge) with the
standard TPID, and the interface hierarchy continues to the conduit as
the ethernet leaf - the same shape as eth0.<vid> on a plain trunk, which
the firmware forwards at line rate. Only the VLAN-specific helpers that
read vlan_dev_priv() are guarded, because such an iface is not an 802.1Q
net device: the egress QoS mask in the NSS frontends, the accel stats
update, and the bridge-VLAN membership check of the VLAN-over-bridge
rule (which otherwise rejected every such rule as unresolvable - the
port's tag is on the wire by construction, so the rule keeps it).

An 802.1Q upper of such a port (wan.35, the usual PPPoE ISP layout) is on
the wire with its own tag only - the switch carries that VLAN through and
the tagger passes it untouched - so the hierarchy steps from the upper
straight to the conduit and the rule gets that one VLAN, the eth0.35
shape, not the port's VID stacked under it.

Compiled out on kernels without CONFIG_NET_DSA (the tag_8021q library is part
of dsa_core); no change for
any device that is not a tag_8021q DSA port.

